# OpenCalc demo — copy to `.env` and edit. Every value has a working default;
# what is here is what an operator actually changes.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one you must change before anybody else can reach this
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the tokens the host mints and the collaboration server verifies. Both
# containers read it, which is why the demo uses a shared secret — a real
# deployment should use OPENCALC_JWKS_URL instead, so the server can verify a
# token and cannot mint one.
# Refused by the server as written: this file is public, so this value is not a
# secret. Generate one, e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`.
OPENCALC_SHARED_SECRET=change-me-before-anyone-else-can-reach-this

# The audience the token must name. Any string, the same on both sides.
OPENCALC_AUDIENCE=opencalc-demo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Addresses. The two that get confused are marked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a BROWSER connects to for co-editing.
#
# **Left empty on purpose, and that is the working setting.** The host derives
# it from the request the browser just made — same origin, `/collab`, and
# `wss://` when the page arrived over TLS — which is the one address known to
# reach it.
#
# This file used to set `ws://127.0.0.1:8443/collab`. That reads like a working
# value and is not one: the collaboration server is `expose`d to the compose
# network and is **not published to your machine**, so the browser dialled a
# port nothing was listening on and co-editing never connected. `docker compose
# up` after `cp .env.example .env` — the documented quick start — produced a
# stack whose one distinguishing feature was broken. The proxy routes `/collab`
# to it on the page's own origin, which is the whole of PROD-12.
#
# Set this only when collaboration lives on a hostname of its own; then it is
# your public URL with `wss://`.
OPENCALC_COLLAB_WS=

# What the COLLABORATION SERVER calls the host — a container name on the compose
# network, not localhost. Getting this wrong is the classic first failure: the
# server fetches `localhost` and finds itself.
OPENCALC_HOST_INTERNAL=http://host:8080

# Where the host is published on your machine.
# The cluster stack reads its own, because it publishes a different port: one
# name shared between the two stacks sends the browser at a port the cluster
# does not expose, and the only symptom is "reconnecting" forever.
OPENCALC_CLUSTER_COLLAB_WS=ws://127.0.0.1:8090/collab
OPENCALC_PROXY_PORT=8090

# Set this to switch on /admin. Empty means there is no admin page at all.
OPENCALC_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The coordinator, in a cluster only
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset here on purpose: the single-node demo is standalone and needs no Redis.
# `docker compose -f docker-compose.cluster.yml up` sets it for you.
#
# **`rediss://` for TLS, and the difference matters.** This link carries the
# lease that decides which node may write a document and every operation
# appended to the log; `redis://` sends all of it in clear and the server says
# so once at startup. Full table: docs/65-RUNNING-IT.md.
#
# **For a replicated coordinator, name the sentinels rather than a box**
# (ADR-020):
#
#   redis+sentinel://host1:26379,host2:26379,host3:26379/opencalc
#
# and `rediss+sentinel://` for the same over TLS. The last path segment is the
# name the sentinels monitor the primary under. The address is then re-asked on
# every failure rather than fixed at startup, which is what makes a failover a
# pause instead of a restart of every node — a promoted primary is found, and so
# is a *demoted* one, which is the case that otherwise looks healthy and refuses
# every write forever.
#
# A private CA cannot be used with a sentinel URL and is refused rather than
# ignored: this build resolves the primary through a path that has nowhere to
# put one. Use a direct `rediss://` URL for a private CA.
OPENCALC_REDIS_URL=

# A PEM CA the coordinator's certificate must chain to, instead of the system
# trust store — which is the usual case, because an internal Redis is not
# issued a certificate by a public authority. Only read with a rediss:// URL:
# certificates against a plaintext URL are refused at startup rather than
# started in clear.
OPENCALC_REDIS_CA=

# This node's own certificate and key, when the coordinator requires one
# (`tls-auth-clients yes`). Both or neither; one without the other is a typo,
# and starting without a client certificate because its path was misspelled is
# the failure nobody notices until Redis starts refusing this node.
OPENCALC_REDIS_CLIENT_CERT=
OPENCALC_REDIS_CLIENT_KEY=

# The prefix every key and channel sits under. Change it when one Redis is
# shared between deployments: two sharing a prefix share leases, and a staging
# node will take leadership of a production document and be believed.
OPENCALC_REDIS_NAMESPACE=opencalc

# **The durability floor, and the one setting that makes a failover safe to
# have** (ADR-020). Redis replication is asynchronous: a primary acknowledges an
# append before any replica has it, so a promotion can drop edits this server
# already told people were saved. `min-replicas-to-write` on the Redis side
# turns that window into a *refusal* — the primary stops accepting writes when
# too few replicas are in sync, clients are told `NotSaving`, and `/readyz`
# answers 503.
#
# Set this to the same number and the node **checks** that its coordinator is
# configured that way — at startup and again after every failover, because the
# setting is per server and the usual mistake is setting it on the original
# primary and nowhere else. A primary below the floor is refused rather than
# used.
#
# Unset means unchecked, which is right for the single-box coordinator this
# file's demo uses: there are no replicas to be in sync with.
OPENCALC_REDIS_MIN_REPLICAS=

OPENCALC_HOST_PORT=8080

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bounds. The defaults are sensible; these are here so you know they exist.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# **Both default to 0, meaning no count limit.** A node is bounded by the memory
# it actually has, not by a number somebody guessed — a guess is wrong in both
# directions at once, turning work away on a large node and accepting more than
# a small one can hold. Set either to a positive number only if you want a hard
# ceiling on top of the memory one.
OPENCALC_MAX_DOCUMENTS=0
OPENCALC_MAX_PARTICIPANTS=0

# What the node may use before it stops admitting new documents and
# participants. Discovered from the container's cgroup limit; set this only if
# your deployment knows better than its cgroup does.
OPENCALC_MEMORY_LIMIT_BYTES=

# The fraction of that limit at which admission stops (50-95).
#
# **It refuses before the ceiling, not at it.** Reaching the cgroup limit is an
# OOM kill, and that takes every document on the node including everybody's
# unsaved work; refusing one new document costs one person a click. Raise it if
# you would rather run closer to the edge.
OPENCALC_MEMORY_HIGH_WATER_PERCENT=85
OPENCALC_IDLE_EVICTION_MS=30000

# How often the signing keys are re-read from OPENCALC_JWKS_URL. Only matters
# when you use a JWKS rather than a shared secret.
#
# A key you *publish* is picked up within seconds without this: a token naming a
# `kid` the server does not hold prompts one re-read, throttled by
# OPENCALC_JWKS_MIN_REFRESH_MS so an invented `kid` cannot make this server
# hammer your key endpoint. This interval is for the other direction — a key you
# *withdraw*, which nothing presents a token for and so nothing else would
# notice. It bounds how long a revoked key keeps working.
# How many connections may be waiting to authenticate at once, and how long
# each may take to send its token. A WebSocket upgrade cannot require a token —
# the token arrives in the first frame — so these are what stop somebody
# opening sockets and never speaking until the node runs out of descriptors.
# Separate from OPENCALC_MAX_PARTICIPANTS, which counts people in a document.
OPENCALC_MAX_PENDING_CONNECTIONS=256
OPENCALC_JOIN_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

OPENCALC_JWKS_REFRESH_MS=300000
OPENCALC_JWKS_MIN_REFRESH_MS=10000

# Plain HTTP on loopback for the demo. Both of these are "local development
# only" and neither is defaulted on in the server.
OPENCALC_ALLOW_PLAIN_CALLBACKS=1
OPENCALC_ALLOWED_HOSTS=host,127.0.0.1,localhost

# Fonts for the headless renderer (thumbnails, PNG export). The editor itself
# never needs these — the browser draws cells with its own faces.
#
# Latin is built in. Drop a .ttf/.otf/.ttc into this directory for any other
# script and it is served and registered at boot; see docs/65-RUNNING-IT.md for
# which face covers which script. Empty is fine and is the normal case.
OPENCALC_FONT_DIR=/fonts

# How many previous versions of a document the host keeps. One is written each
# time the document is saved, so this bounds what a day of editing costs in
# disk. Set it to 0 to keep none.
OPENCALC_MAX_VERSIONS=20

# The largest workbook the host accepts, in bytes (default 64 MB). The web
# framework's own default is 2 MB, which is smaller than many real spreadsheets.
OPENCALC_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=67108864

# --- The WOPI adapter (docker compose --profile wopi) -------------------------
#
# What makes OpenCalc installable into Nextcloud, ownCloud, SharePoint, Moodle
# and Alfresco: an administrator pastes OPENCALC_WOPI_PUBLIC_URL/hosting/discovery
# into their file store's settings and OpenCalc appears in the editor list.
# Design and the full handshake: docs/74-WOPI-INTEGRATION.md.

# **Required, with no default, and the process will not start without it.**
# A WOPISrc arrives in a query string, so it is chosen by whoever wrote the link
# the user clicked. Without a list this service fetches whatever that link says
# — a server-side request forgery with a friendly interface. Comma separated,
# hostnames only.
OPENCALC_WOPI_ALLOWED_HOSTS=nextcloud.local

# Where a *browser* reaches the adapter. It goes into the discovery document,
# so it is the address the file store sends people to; behind a proxy it is
# never the bind address.
OPENCALC_WOPI_PUBLIC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8090
OPENCALC_WOPI_PORT=8090

# How long an editing session — and the WOPI lock it holds — lives (8 hours).
OPENCALC_WOPI_SESSION_TTL_MS=28800000
OPENCALC_WOPI_MAX_SESSIONS=500

# White-labelling. Unset means OpenCalc's own name and colours; setting the
# name changes the editor list entry, the browser tab and the toolbar.
OPENCALC_BRAND_NAME=
OPENCALC_BRAND_FAVICON_URL=
OPENCALC_BRAND_ACCENT=
